#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[Industry watch, September 2026]

- *Frontier models score highly on textbook analog questions.* The top Razavi-Bench model scored 95.50%, graded by another language model, and the benchmark has an experimental simulator-assisted mode << cite('razavi_bench_2026') >>. A bench tool must add what textbooks do not test: tolerances, ambiguity between faults, the cost of each measurement and safety. T5 compares the full system with such a model working alone.
- *IBM Maximo 9.2 (June 2026)* adds agentic workflows and an MCP server through which customers can connect their own agents << cite('ibm_mas_92_2026') >>, a possible route for a diagnosis engine into industrial repair depots.
- *EU AI Act transparency rules apply from 2 August 2026* << cite('ec_ip_26_1714') >>; the 2026 amendments moved the Annex III high-risk rules to 2 December 2027 << cite('eu_ai_omnibus_2026_1744') >> and keep a duty on providers and deployers to support their staff's AI literacy << cite('eu_ai_act_consolidated_2026') >>. They would bind Differential only once offered in the EU.
- *Colorado re-enacted its AI law* as a regime for automated decision-making technology in consequential decisions, approved on 14 May 2026 and in force from 1 January 2027 << cite('co_sb26_189') >>. A shop that used repair tickets to decide about its staff could come within such rules; Differential's license forbids that use.
- *Claude Sonnet 5.5 was released on 28 September 2026* << cite('claude_sonnet_5_5_overview') >>. Models change quickly, so Differential pins its model, caches responses and re-runs its safety and grounding targets (T15-T19) on any change.

